The extension_RequirePasswordChange attribute is often used in Azure AD B2C custom policies to enforce a password change during the user's next login. This attribute is not built into Azure AD B2C by default but can be added as a custom attribute and used within custom policies.

Here’s how to implement extension_RequirePasswordChange in Azure AD B2C:


---

Step 1: Add the Custom Attribute

1. Go to the Azure AD B2C portal.


2. Navigate to Azure AD B2C > User Attributes.


3. Click + Add to create a new custom attribute:

Name: RequirePasswordChange

Data Type: Boolean



4. Save the attribute.




---

Step 2: Modify the Custom Policy

1. Download the starter pack for custom policies if you don’t already have it: Azure AD B2C Custom Policy Starter Pack.


2. Open the TrustFrameworkExtensions.xml file and add the following:



Define the Custom Attribute in the Claim Schema

Add the attribute to the claim schema:

<ClaimType Id="extension_RequirePasswordChange">
  <DisplayName>Require Password Change</DisplayName>
  <DataType>boolean</DataType>
  <DefaultValue>false</DefaultValue>
  <UserHelpText>Indicates if the user must change their password.</UserHelpText>
  <UserInputType>Checkbox</UserInputType>
</ClaimType>

Modify the User Journey

In your sign-in journey, add a step to check the extension_RequirePasswordChange claim and redirect users to a password reset screen if it’s set to true.

Example:

Add this step in the OrchestrationSteps section:

<OrchestrationStep Order="2" Type="ClaimsExchange">
  <Preconditions>
    <Precondition Type="ClaimEquals" ExecuteActionsIf="true">
      <Value>extension_RequirePasswordChange</Value>
      <Value>true</Value>
      <Action>SkipThisOrchestrationStep</Action>
    </Precondition>
  </Preconditions>
  <ClaimsExchanges>
    <ClaimsExchange Id="PasswordReset" TechnicalProfileReferenceId="PasswordResetProfile" />
  </ClaimsExchanges>
</OrchestrationStep>

Define the Technical Profile:

Add a technical profile for password reset if it doesn’t exist:

<TechnicalProfile Id="PasswordResetProfile">
  <Metadata>
    <Item Key="Operation">ChangePassword</Item>
  </Metadata>
  <InputClaims>
    <InputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="email" />
  </InputClaims>
  <OutputClaims>
    <OutputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="newPassword" />
    <OutputClaim ClaimTypeReferenceId="objectId" Required="true" />
  </OutputClaims>
</TechnicalProfile>


---

Step 3: Assign the Attribute to Users

You can assign the extension_RequirePasswordChange attribute to users via:

1. Azure portal: Update user attributes manually.


2. PowerShell or Microsoft Graph API: Automate the process of setting this attribute.



Example with PowerShell:

Connect-MgGraph -Scopes "User.ReadWrite.All"
Update-MgUser -UserId <UserId> -AdditionalProperties @{"extension_RequirePasswordChange"=$true}


---

Step 4: Test the Policy

1. Upload the updated policy files (TrustFrameworkExtensions.xml, etc.) to Azure AD B2C.


2. Test the policy by setting extension_RequirePasswordChange to true for a user and logging in with that user.




---

Expected Behavior

1. When a user logs in, the policy checks if extension_RequirePasswordChange is true.


2. If true, the user is redirected to a password change screen.


3. After changing the password, the attribute is reset to false (this needs to be configured in the policy or managed manually).

