The extension_RequirePasswordChange attribute can be managed using the Microsoft Graph API if you’ve already set up the custom attribute in your Azure AD B2C tenant. Here’s a step-by-step guide to use the Graph API to set or manage this attribute:


---

Prerequisites

1. Register an Application in Azure AD B2C:

Go to Azure AD B2C > App registrations.

Click New registration.

Assign API permissions:

Microsoft Graph:

User.ReadWrite.All

Directory.AccessAsUser.All



Grant admin consent for the permissions.

Generate a client secret.



2. Install Microsoft Graph SDK (Optional): If using PowerShell or .NET SDK, install the Graph SDK:

Install-Module -Name Microsoft.Graph


3. Add the Custom Attribute: Ensure the extension_RequirePasswordChange attribute is already created under Azure AD B2C > User Attributes.




---

Step 1: Authenticate with Microsoft Graph API

Use the Graph API to get an access token. Here's an example using PowerShell:

PowerShell:

# Authenticate to Azure AD
Connect-MgGraph -Scopes "User.ReadWrite.All Directory.AccessAsUser.All"

Or Use Postman:

POST to https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenantId}/oauth2/v2.0/token

Body:

grant_type=client_credentials
client_id=<client-id>
client_secret=<client-secret>
scope=https://graph.microsoft.com/.default



---

Step 2: Set the extension_RequirePasswordChange Attribute

Update User Attribute via Graph API:

Endpoint:

PATCH https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/users/{user-id}

Headers:

Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {access-token}

Body:

{
  "extension_<appId>_RequirePasswordChange": true
}

Replace <appId> with the Application ID of your Azure AD B2C application, formatted without dashes.


Example PowerShell:

Update-MgUser -UserId "<user-id>" -AdditionalProperties @{"extension_<appId>_RequirePasswordChange"=$true}


---

Step 3: Verify the Attribute

You can verify that the attribute was set successfully:

GET User:

Endpoint:

GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/users/{user-id}

Look for the extension_<appId>_RequirePasswordChange field in the response.



---

Step 4: Handle the Attribute in Custom Policies

In your custom policy, ensure the extension_RequirePasswordChange attribute triggers the password change workflow.


---

Notes

Replace <appId> with your app registration’s Application ID (formatted without dashes).

Ensure proper permissions are granted to the app or user executing these Graph API calls.


